= Modules

#statement("Definition")[
Ring $R$ is a preaddtive categor y with a single object $bullet$ \ A #emph[left] $R$-#emph[module]#index-entry("Left R module") an additive functor from $R$ to the category of abelian groups, $bullet$ is mapped to $M$ which is an abelian group, $r$ are mapped to endomorphisms of $M$ which induces a left group action $R times M arrow.r M$, $\(r\,m\)mapsto r m$

+ $1 m = m$

+ $\(r s\)m = r\(s m\)$

+ $r\(m + n\)= r m + r n$

+ $\(r + s\)m = r m + s m$

1 and 2 are given by functoriality, 3 is given by the linearity of $r$, 4 is given by additivity \ A #emph[right] $R$-#emph[module]#index-entry("Right R module") an additive functor from $R^(upright(o p))$ to the category of abelian groups, inducing right group action $M times R arrow.r M$, $\(m\,r\)mapsto m r$

+ $m = m 1$

+ $m\(r s\)=\(m r\)s$

+ $\(m + n\)r = m r + n r$

+ $m\(r + s\)= m r + m s$

1 and 2 are given by functoriality, 3 is given by the linearity of $r$, 4 is given by additivity

]#label("Module")
#statement("Definition")[
A morphism between left $R$-modules $phi.alt : M arrow.r N$ is natural transformation

+ $phi.alt\(m_1 + m_2\)= phi.alt\(m_1\)+ phi.alt\(m_2\)$

+ $phi.alt\(r m\)= r phi.alt\(m\)$

#align(center)[
#align(center)[#set text(size:9pt)
#diagram(spacing:(7mm,9mm),
node((0,0),[$M$]),
node((1,0),[$M$]),
node((0,1),[$N$]),
node((1,1),[$N$]),
edge((0,0),(1,0),"->",[$r$]),
edge((0,0),(0,1),"->",[$phi.alt$]),
edge((1,0),(1,1),"->",[$phi.alt$]),
edge((0,1),(1,1),"->",[$r$]))]
]
1 is given by the linearity of $phi.alt$, 2 is given by naturality

]
#statement("Definition")[
$X subset.eq M$ is #emph[linearly independent]#index-entry("Linearly independent") if for any $x_1\,dots.h.c\,x_n in X$ #display-equation($r_1 x_1 + dots.h.c + r_n x_n = 0 arrow.r.double r_i = 0$)

]
#statement("Definition")[
The submodule generated by $X subset.eq M$ is $"Span" X$, the #emph[span]#index-entry("span") of $X$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$X subset.eq M$ is a #emph[basis]#index-entry("Basis of a module") of $M$ if $X$ is a linearly independent spanning set. $M$ is a free $R$ module on $X$ if $X$ is a basis of $M$

]
#aside("Note")[
There is no well-defined dimension for free $R$ modules in general, examplified in Example #ref(label("R^2=R"), supplement: none)

]
#statement("Definition")[
$M$ is an right $R$-module, $N$ is a left $R$-module and $G$ is an abelian group, a map $phi.alt : M times N arrow.r G$ is called an $R$ balanced product if $phi.alt$ is bilinear and $phi.alt\(m r\,n\)= phi.alt\(m\,r n\)$, we can define tensor product $M times.o_R N$ is an abelian group satisfying the universal property

#align(center)[
#align(center)[#set text(size:9pt)
#diagram(spacing:(7mm,9mm),
node((0,0),[$M times N$]),
node((1,0),[$M times.o_R N$]),
node((1,1),[$G$]),
edge((0,0),(1,1),"->",[$f$]),
edge((0,0),(1,0),"->",[$times.o$]),
edge((1,0),(1,1),"->",[$exists_1 tilde(f)$],stroke:(dash:"dashed")))]
]
Here $f$ is an $R$ balanced product and $tilde(f)$ is an abelian group homomorphism

A concrete construction would be $F\(M times N\)\/tilde.op$, where $\(m + m'\,n\)tilde.op\(m\,n\)+\(m'\,n\)$, $\(m\,n + n'\)tilde.op\(m\,n\)+\(m\,n'\)$, $\(m r\,n\)tilde.op\(m\,r n\)$

]
#aside("Remark")[
$r\(m times.o n\)= m r times.o n = m times.o r n$ is called associativity

]
#statement("Definition")[
Module $M$ is #emph[semisimple] or #emph[completely reducible] if it is the direct sum of simple submodules. Ring $R$ is semisimple if it is a semisimple $R$ module

]
#statement("Theorem")[
Tensor product is right exact for $R$ modules

]
#statement("Definition")[
$M$ is a #emph[flat]#index-entry("Flat module") $R$ module if $"Tor"_1^R\(M\,-\)$ is exact

]
#statement("Definition")[
$S subset.eq R$ is #emph[multiplicatively closed]#index-entry("Multiplicatively closed") if $1 = s^0 in S$ and $r s in S\,forall r\,s in S$, we can define localization $S^(- 1) R$ satisfying universal property

#align(center)[
#align(center)[#set text(size:9pt)
#diagram(spacing:(7mm,9mm),
node((0,0),[$R$]),
node((0,1),[$S^(- 1) R$]),
node((1,1),[$T$]),
edge((0,0),(1,1),"->",[$f$]),
edge((0,0),(0,1),"->",[$j$]),
edge((0,1),(1,1),"->",[$exists_1 g$],stroke:(dash:"dashed")))]
]
Here $f\(S\)subset.eq T^times$

Concrete construction: $S^(- 1) R := R times S\/tilde.op$, $\(r\,s\)tilde.op\(r'\,s'\)$ if there exists $t in S$ such that $t\(r s' - r' s\)= 0$

Let $M$ be an $R$ module, we can define localization, $S^(- 1) M := M times S\/tilde.op$, $\(m\,s\)tilde.op\(m'\,s'\)$ if there exists $t in S$ such that $t\(s' m - s m'\)= 0$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Suppose $0 arrow.r A arrow.r^f B arrow.r^g C arrow.r 0$ is a short exact sequence, then it is obvious that $A times.o D arrow.r^(f times.o 1_D) B times.o D arrow.r^(g times.o 1_D) C times.o D arrow.r 0$ is a complex and $g times.o 1_D$ is surjective, now define $phi.alt : B times.o D\/ker g times.o 1_D arrow.r A times.o D$, $b times.o d mapsto a times.o d$, where $a$ is the unique element in $A$ such that $g\(b - f\(a\)\)= 0$

]
#statement("Definition")[
Let $P_i\,A\,D$ be $R$ modules, $dots.h.c P_2 arrow.r^(d_2) P_1 arrow.r^(d_1) P_0 arrow.r^epsilon.alt A arrow.r 0$ be a projective resolution, then we have $0 arrow.r H o m_R\(A\,D\)arrow.r^epsilon.alt H o m_R\(P_0\,D\)arrow.r^(d_1) H o m_R\(P_1\,D\)arrow.r^(d_2) H o m_R\(P_2\,D\)dots.h.c$, define $E x t_R^n\(A\,D\)$ to be the $n$-th cohomology group of $0 arrow.r H o m_R\(P_0\,D\)arrow.r^(d_1) H o m_R\(P_1\,D\)arrow.r^(d_2) H o m_R\(P_2\,D\)dots.h.c$, note that $E x t_R^0\(A\,D\)tilde.equiv H o m_R\(A\,D\)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
Let $P_i\,B\,D$ be $R$ modules, $dots.h.c P_2 arrow.r^(d_2) P_1 arrow.r^(d_1) P_0 arrow.r^epsilon.alt B arrow.r 0$ be a projective resolution, then we have$dots.h.c D times.o_R P_2 arrow.r^(1 times.o d_2) D times.o_R P_1 arrow.r^(1 times.o d_1) D times.o_R P_0 arrow.r^(1 times.o epsilon.alt) D times.o_R B arrow.r 0$, define $T o r_n^R\(D\,B\)$ to be the $n$-th homology group of $dots.h.c D times.o_R P_2 arrow.r^(1 times.o d_2) D times.o_R P_1 arrow.r^(1 times.o d_1) D times.o_R P_0 arrow.r 0$, note that $T o r_0^R\(D\,B\)tilde.equiv D times.o_R B$

]
#statement("Lemma", title: [Schur's Lemma])[
#index-entry("Schur’s lemma") $M\,N$ are nonzero simple $R$ modules. A homomorphism $phi : M arrow.r N$ is either $0$ or an isomorphism. In particular, $"End"_R\(M\)$ is a division ring. Moreover, if $F$ is algebraically closed, $"Hom"_F\(M\,N\)= { lambda phi\|lambda in F }$ where $M arrow.r^phi N$ is an isomorphism(all isomorphisms are scalar multiple of each other), in particular, $"Hom"_F\(M\,M\)= { lambda 1_M\|lambda in F }$

]#label("Schur's lemma")
#statement("Theorem", title: [Maschke's theorem])[
$G$ is a finite group, $F$ is a field, $upright(c h a r) F divides.not\|G\|$, then $F G$ is a semisimple ring

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Artin-Wedderburn theorem])[
$R = V_1 xor dots.h.c xor V_r$ is a semisimple ring, by Schur's lemma #ref(label("Schur's lemma"), supplement: none), $D_i = "End"_R\(V_i\)$ are division rings, then #display-equation($R tilde.equiv M_(n_1)\(D_1\)times dots.h.c times M_(n_r)\(D_r\)$) where $n_i = dim_(D_i)\(V_i\)$. $sum_(i = 1)^r n_i^2 =\|G\|$, $r$ is the number of conjugacy classes in $G$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$F$ is a field, $G$ is a group, the representation ring $R_F\(G\)$ is the completion of the set of isomorphic classes of representations

]
#statement("Definition")[
The symmetric $k$ algebra is $S^k\(V\)subset.eq T^k\(V\)$ consists of $k$ tensors symmetric under the permutation of $S_k$. The exterior $k$ algebra is $and.big^k\(V\)subset.eq T^k\(V\)$ consists of $k$ tensors antisymmetric under the permutation of $S_k$. We have projections #display-equation($"Sym" : T^k\(V\)arrow.r T^k\(V\)\,thin a_1 times.o dots.h.c times.o a_k mapsto frac(1, k !) sum_sigma a_(sigma\(1\)) times.o dots.h.c times.o a_(sigma\(n\))$) and #display-equation($"Alt" : T^k\(V\)arrow.r T^k\(V\)\,thin a_1 times.o dots.h.c times.o a_k mapsto frac(1, k !) sum_sigma\(- 1\)^("sgn" sigma) a_(sigma\(1\)) times.o dots.h.c times.o a_(sigma\(n\))$) For $alpha in T^k\(V\)$, $beta in T^l\(V\)$, define #display-equation($alpha beta = alpha dot.o beta = "Sym"\(alpha times.o beta\)$) #display-equation($alpha and beta = frac(\(k + l\)!, k ! l !) "Alt"\(alpha times.o beta\)$) Which corresponds to determinants

]
#statement("Lemma", title: [Nakayama's lemma])[
$M$ is a finitely generated $R$ module, $I subset.eq R$ is an ideal, $phi.alt in "End"_R\(M\)$, if $phi.alt\(M\)subset.eq I M$, then #display-equation($phi.alt^n + a_1 phi.alt^(n - 1) + dots.h.c + a_n = 0$) for some $a_i in I$. In particular If $I M = M$, then there exists $r equiv 1 #h(0.444em) mod med I$ such that $r M = 0$

]#label("Nakayama's lemma")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Consider multiplication by scalars as endomorphisms, $1 M subset.eq M = I M$ is satisfied, thus $1 + a_1 + dots.h.c + a_n = 0$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
Localization is exact. i.e. If $0 arrow.r A arrow.r B arrow.r C arrow.r 0$ is exact, so is $0 arrow.r S^(- 1) A arrow.r S^(- 1) B arrow.r S^(- 1) C arrow.r 0$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[

]
